<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Posidonius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 135–c. 51 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Posidonius of Rhodes, Posidonius of Apamea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Posidonius was a Greek Stoic philosopher, historian and scientist from Apamea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> who settled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, where he ran a famous school and held public office. He wrote on philosophy, history, geography, astronomy and the tides, and estimated the circumference of the Earth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> visited him, and his works, now lost, influenced many later writers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/posidonius.jpg" alt="Posidonius" width="320" height="586" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Posidonio,_replica_augustea_(23_ac.-14_dc_ca)_da_originale_del_100-50_ac._ca._6142.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The orator Aeschines is said to have taught there in exile, the Stoic philosophers Panaetius and <b>Posidonius</b> were closely associated with the island, and Apollonius Molon taught rhetoric to Cicero.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Pliny the Elder, Lucan, Strabo, <b>Posidonius</b> and Diodorus Siculus added rites and sacrifices, but such writers saw the Celts as “barbarians”, and the Romans had reason to make them seem uncivilised.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Posidonius</b>, a Greek who travelled in Gaul about 100 BCE and whose account survives through Diodorus Siculus, Strabo and Athenaeus, wrote of feasts at which the bravest man claimed the best cut of meat and rivals fought him for it, and Diodorus says that Italian merchants could get…</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apamea-syria">Apamea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.42,36.4&amp;year=-81" aria-label="Apamea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5471 884 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Posidonius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5921 1154h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5921 1154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.42,36.4&amp;year=-81">What was here in 81 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
